# One failing test per function `core/testing/assert` exports, so that the
# message each produces is recorded rather than assumed. The seventeen of them
# take three shapes:
#
#   * `eq`, `notEq`, `contains`, `isEmpty`, `len`, `gt`, `ge`, `lt`, `le` and
#     `approxEq` render both sides — which is what the widened set is *for*:
#     `isTrue(xs.contains(x))` printed "expected true, got false" and named
#     neither the list nor the element;
#   * `isTrue`, `isFalse`, `ok`, `err`, `some` and `none` render the value they
#     got against a constant — `true`, `.Ok`, `.Some`;
#   * `notEmpty` is a sentence with no diff, because the only value it can fail
#     at is the empty list and `actual: []` against `expected: []` would be two
#     ways of writing it.
#
# There is no `fail` any more. It carried the test's own words and returned
# `()`, so a failing match arm could not produce a value and a test fabricated
# a dummy one to type-check; a test that has to fail now says which assertion
# failed.
#
# The last two runs are `eq` on an `Option` and on a `Result`, which is the
# *value* rendering of the same two types the four unwrapping assertions name
# — the pair is here so a change to one and not the other shows up.
#
# `assert.some` and `assert.none` show the `Option` itself — `.None` against
# `.Some`, `.Some(3)` against `.None` — so both sides of the report are the
# value in the syntax it is written in. `some` used to pass the literal string
# `"none"`, which was indistinguishable from a test whose value really was
# `.Some("none")`.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "every assertion core/testing/assert exports, failing, with the message each produces"

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/asserts", "--force", "--output=js"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "fail.txt"
  stream: OUT
}
